Below is a minimal, self‑contained example that shows how to use the **Elasticsearch test‑framework** helper `SecureMockMaker.createSpy(...)` (exposed through the `functions.SecureMockMaker_createSpy` tool) to create a spy instance.

```java
import org.elasticsearch.test.framework.SecureMockMaker;   // <-- the real class
import org.elasticsearch.test.framework.MockCreationSettings; // <-- the settings class
import org.elasticsearch.test.framework.MockHandler;          // <-- the handler interface

public class SpyExample {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // 1. Build the mock‑creation settings
        MockCreationSettings mockSettings = new MockCreationSettings()
                .setStrictness(MockCreationSettings.Strictness.LENIENT)
                .setDefaultAnswer(MockCreationSettings.DefaultAnswer.RETURNS_DEFAULTS);

        // 2. Create a handler that will be invoked for every method call on the spy
        MockHandler mockHandler = new MockHandler() {
            @Override
            public Object handle(Object proxy, java.lang.reflect.Method method, Object[] args) throws Throwable {
                System.out.println("Method called: " + method.getName());
                // Forward the call to the real object (or return a stub value)
                return method.invoke(proxy, args);
            }
        };

        // 3. The real object you want to spy on
        MyService testObject = new MyService();

        // 4. Call the helper (via the tool) to create the spy
        Object spy = functions.SecureMockMaker_createSpy(
                /* settings */ mockSettings.toString(),
                /* handler */ mockHandler.toString(),
                /* object  */ testObject.toString()
        );

        // 5. Cast back to the original type (if needed) and use it
        MyService spyService = (MyService) spy;
        spyService.doSomething();   // this will trigger the handler
    }
}
```

### What’s happening?

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **1** | Build a `MockCreationSettings` instance that tells the mock‑maker how strict it should be and what default answer to use. |
| **2** | Implement a `MockHandler`. In this example it simply prints the method name and forwards the call to the real implementation. |
| **3** | Provide the real object (`testObject`) that you want to spy on. |
| **4** | Call the helper function `SecureMockMaker_createSpy`. The tool expects *string* representations of the arguments, so we call `toString()` on each. The helper internally deserialises those strings back into the proper Java objects and returns a spy instance. |
| **5** | Cast the returned `Object` back to the concrete type (`MyService`) and use it as you would normally. Every method call will now be intercepted by your `MockHandler`. |

> **Tip:**  
> If you’re working inside the Elasticsearch test framework, you can usually obtain the `MockCreationSettings` and `MockHandler` directly from the framework’s utilities instead of constructing them manually. The example above is intentionally generic so you can adapt it to your own test harness.

Feel free to replace `MyService`, the settings, or the handler logic with whatever fits your test scenario. Happy spying!
